const TEACHING = "Run the Team (Pack 3, level L3, version 1.1.0)\nPromise: Turn what only you can see into instructions the players in front of you act on.\n\n## Running the Team — Section 06\nYou see everything. Nobody else on the pitch does. A keeper who turns what he sees into clear instructions prevents more goals than any keeper who just saves shots. This is the section that raises your bar.\n\nHere's the thing most young keepers don't realise: the defenders in front of you are often guessing. They have their back to the goal, an attacker in their face, and no idea where the runner behind them is. You know. You can see the runner, the gap in the line, the winger ghosting into the back post, the full-back who's been dragged out. If you say nothing, that knowledge is worthless. If you say the right thing early, the danger never becomes a shot.\nOrganising is a skill with technique, exactly like diving. It has a method, it can be practised, and at this level it is the single biggest thing that separates keepers who get noticed from keepers who don't.\n\nHow a good instruction works\n1. Name — Who it's for. 'Thabo —'. A call without a name is noise; everyone assumes it's for someone else.\n2. Instruction — One verb. 'Step.' 'Drop.' 'Tight.' 'Tuck in.' Not a paragraph — a word they can act on without thinking.\n3. Direction — Where, if it isn't obvious. 'Left shoulder.' 'Back post.' 'Behind you.'\n4. Confirm — Did they do it? If yes, 'Good.' If no, say it again louder. Then move on — don't nag.\n\nThe rules of a voice people follow\n- Early beats loud — tag: Timing · A whisper at the right moment beats a scream too late. The instruction must arrive before the problem does — when you see the shape forming, not when the ball is already in the space.\n- Calm, clear, certain — tag: Tone · Shouting in panic makes everyone panic. A short, confident instruction makes everyone confident. Your voice sets the temperature of the whole defence.\n- Instruct, never complain — tag: Content · 'Why didn't you track him?!' changes nothing. 'Next one — go with the runner.' changes the next one. Fix the future, not the past.\n- Constant, not constant shouting — tag: Volume · Be talking the whole time they're attacking, in short bursts. Save the loud voice for the three calls that matter most: 'Keeper's!', 'Away!', and the one that stops a goal.\n- Praise is an instruction too — tag: Ownership · 'Good, Sipho — again.' tells him what to keep doing. Defenders work harder for a keeper who notices.\n- Earn it with your own play — tag: Credibility · They follow the voice of a keeper who is sure and who takes responsibility. Call it, do it, own it — and they'll trust you with the next one.\n\nBuilding the habit\nYou won't learn thirty calls at once. Pick three for your next match — probably Keeper's!, Away!, and Step up! — and use them every single time the situation comes up. When they're automatic, add Tight!, Back post! and Man on!. Then the shape calls. Within a season, the whole sheet becomes instinct.\nSay the calls out loud in training too, even in drills, even when they feel unnecessary. The voice is a muscle.\n\nIf you're not sure what to say: Say where the danger is. 'Runner, left!' 'Space behind!' 'Edge of the box!' You don't need the perfect instruction — you need to transfer what you can see to the people who can't. Information first, instruction when you've got it.\n## Match Day — Section 09\nPerformance starts before the whistle and doesn't end at it. A routine turns nerves into focus and a match into learning.\n\nThe match, phase by phase\n- Before — The day before: pack your kit, drink water, sleep. Spend five minutes with this guide on one section — not the whole thing.\nArriving: walk the pitch. Is it bouncy, wet, long grass? Which way is the sun? Which goal is it in which half? Is the wind behind you or against you? These change your decisions — a wet pitch means parry wide, don't catch low drives; a sun in your eyes means come for fewer crosses.\nWarm-up: handling, footwork, a few crosses, a few shots, two or three kicks. Then voice: start talking in the warm-up. You should be organising the team before the game starts.\n- First five minutes — Get a touch early — a back pass, a catch, anything. Make your first decision a safe one. Say something to every defender by name in the first few minutes. Set the tone: you're switched on, calm, loud.\n- During — Scan constantly. Move on every pass. Talk in every defensive phase. One breath after every mistake, then the next job. Keep an eye on the clock and the score — the way you play at 1–0 up with ten minutes left is different from 0–0 at kick-off.\n- Half-time — Drink. One honest question to yourself: what's happened in front of me that I could have changed with a call? Tell the coach one thing you've seen about their attack. Tell the defence one thing you want from the second half.\n- After — Shake hands, no drama, win or lose. Then — same day, not next week — do the self-review below. It takes five minutes and it's the single most valuable thing you can do for your development.\n\nReading the opposition\n- Watch their strikers — tag: In the warm-up · Which foot? Do they shoot early or take a touch? Does their winger cross first-time or cut inside? You'll know more than your defenders do by kick-off.\n- How do they attack? — tag: First ten minutes · Over the top? Through the middle? Wide and cross? Once you see the pattern, tell your defence — 'they're going long, drop a yard', 'they're crossing, watch the back post'.\n- Where do they go? — tag: Set pieces · First corner: near post or far? Short or long? Remember it. The second one will probably be the same.\n## The Ladder — Section 10\nWhere you are, where you're going, and what the next rung looks like. Be honest about the level — the ladder only works if you are.\n\nMost U13 keepers with good coaching are at level 2, moving towards 3. The whole point of this guide is level 3 to level 4 — the step from reading the game to running it. That step is mostly voice, and voice is mostly courage. Start small, start now.\n\n\"The keeper who sees early just has to be in the right place.\" — Section 01\n\nWhere to go from here\n- Pick three calls from the Call Sheet. Use them in your next match until they're automatic.\n- Do the post-match review every match for a month. Read them back at the end of it.\n- Watch a professional keeper for a full match — not the highlights. Watch his feet when the ball is far away and watch how often he talks.\n- Ask your coach to tell you one thing per session about what you saw, not what you did.\n## On the Pitch — On the pitch\nVoice is a muscle. Nothing in this pack is real until your defenders have heard it. These tasks make the Call Sheet happen on grass.\n\nVoice is a muscle. Nothing in this pack is real until your defenders have heard it. These tasks make the Call Sheet happen on grass.\nCheck in after each session or match — a date, did it happen (yes, partly, no — all honest answers), and what you noticed. The record is yours. Nobody is checking; the point is that you are.\n\nWhy this pack can't be finished indoors: Reading, deciding and organising are habits, and habits are built on grass. The lessons and the simulator show you what good looks like; only matches and sessions make it yours. Completing a pack means it has shown up in your game — that is the whole point.";
const CLIENT = "Visuals on the site (client-only; decisions made there arrive as events in the shared document, not through a tool):\n- call sheet \"The Call Sheet — what you see, what you say\" → #s/run-the-team/organising/call-sheet\n- checklist \"Organising habits\" (6 items) → #s/run-the-team/organising/organising-habits\n- understanding ladder \"Game-understanding ladder\" → #s/run-the-team/ladder/understanding-ladder";
const FIELD = "Field tasks (#s/run-the-team/on-the-pitch/field-run-the-team). Check-ins are honest self-reports; no proof, no policing.\n- three-calls (do, match): Pick three calls from the Call Sheet before the match (start with Keeper's!, Away!, Step up!). Use them every single time the situation comes up. After the match, count how many times you did and how many you missed.\n  why: You won't learn thirty calls at once. Three, used every time, become instinct in a month — then you add three more.\n  ask: Which three? Roughly how many used vs missed? Which one felt most awkward?\n- name-first (do, session): In every game at training, no instruction without a name in front of it. \"Thabo — step.\" \"Musa — tight.\"\n  why: A call without a name is noise; everyone assumes it's for someone else.\n  ask: Did anyone respond faster when you used their name? Whose name did you keep forgetting?\n- shape-calls (do, match): Give at least one shape call — \"step up\", \"drop\", \"tuck in\", \"squeeze\" — in every defensive phase of the match, not just at set pieces.\n  why: Your defenders are guessing about the line. You are the only one who can see it from behind.\n  ask: Did the line actually move when you called? When did you see it wrong and say nothing?\n- after-mistake (do, match): The first thing you say to a teammate after his mistake must be about the next ball, never the last one. \"Next one, Josh.\"\n  why: A defender thinking about his last mistake makes the next one. You can fix his next thirty seconds.\n  ask: Did it happen? What did you say? What did you want to say?\n- first-voice (do, match): If you concede, be the first voice within five seconds — one instruction, forward-looking, to the whole team.\n  why: The thirty seconds after a goal is when the next one is most likely. The team looks to you.\n  ask: Did you concede? What did you say first? How long did it take you?\n- opposition-read (notice, match): In the first ten minutes, work out how they attack — over the top, through the middle, or wide and cross — and tell your defence one sentence about it.\n  why: You know more than your defenders do by kick-off if you watch the warm-up and the first ten minutes.\n  ask: What was their pattern? Did you tell anyone? Were you right?\n\nDrills (#s/run-the-team/on-the-pitch/drills-run-the-team):\n- say-it \"Say it out loud\" (solo, 6 min)\n  setup: The Call Sheet open, or a highlights clip of any match on your phone.\n  steps: Watch a defensive phase (or read a \"when you see\" row). Say the call out loud — full voice, the way you would on the pitch, with a name in front of it. / If it came out quiet or hesitant, say it again louder. The voice is a muscle. / Ten calls a day for a week before a match.\n  focus: Short, certain, early. Not a paragraph — one verb.\n  game link: Defenders follow a voice that sounds sure. This is where it gets sure.\n- organise-corners \"Run the corner\" (group, 10 min)\n  setup: A corner-kick set-up at training: a taker, six attackers, your defenders, you in goal. Coach watching.\n  steps: Before each kick you organise everything: posts, zones, every attacker named to a defender. The kick does not happen until you say so. / Coach plants one unmarked attacker each time; your job is to find him and fix it before the kick. / Six corners each side. Then swap: a teammate organises and you watch for what he misses.\n  focus: No kick while a man is free. Find him, name him, then set.\n  game link: Corner — Free Man in the simulator, with real voices.\n- blind-defender \"Blind defender\" (pair, 8 min)\n  setup: A partner as a defender with his back to you, facing an attacker (a cone or a third player) 10m from goal. You in goal.\n  steps: Only you can see where the attacker (or a second cone, the \"runner\") is. Your partner must act on your voice alone: \"tight\", \"show him wide\", \"runner left — go\". / Move the cones between reps; the defender keeps his eyes forward. / Ten reps, then swap roles so you feel what it is like to depend on the keeper's voice.\n  focus: Name, one verb, direction. Early beats loud.\n  game link: Your defenders really are this blind to what's behind them. That is why your voice matters.\n\nReview \"Post-match self-review\" (match-review) → #s/run-the-team/matchday/match-review. Prompts:\n  1. Match, score, and what kind of game it was (fast, scrappy, one-sided, tight).\n  2. Three moments I read well — what I saw, what I did.\n  3. One moment I saw late — what cue I missed, and what I'd look for next time.\n  4. Each goal we conceded (if any): where it came from, and the call or action that could have stopped it.\n  5. My voice: what I organised well, and one call I should have made and didn't.\n  6. With the ball: did I pick fast or slow correctly? Any giveaways?\n  7. One thing to work on in training this week.";

export const checkinTool = defineTool({
	name: "checkin_run-the-team",
	description: "Record a field check-in for the Run the Team pack after a session or match. One check-in per task per date. Tasks: three-calls, name-first, shape-calls, after-mistake, first-voice, opposition-read.",
	parameters: Type.Object({
		task: Type.Union([Type.Literal("three-calls"), Type.Literal("name-first"), Type.Literal("shape-calls"), Type.Literal("after-mistake"), Type.Literal("first-voice"), Type.Literal("opposition-read")]),
		date: Type.String({ description: "ISO date (YYYY-MM-DD) of the session or match" }),
		done: Type.Union([Type.Literal("yes"), Type.Literal("partly"), Type.Literal("no")]),
		notes: Type.Optional(Type.String()),
	}),
	execute: async (args, api, context) => {
		const keeperId = keeperIdOf({ conversationId: api.conversationId, agent: await api.agent(context) });
		const event = await api.commit((tx) => appendEvent(tx, keeperId, "field.checkin", { task: args.task, date: args.date, done: args.done, notes: args.notes ?? "" }, CTX), context);
		return { content: [{ type: "text", text: `recorded field.checkin ${args.task} ${args.date} ${args.done} (t=${event.t})` }] };
	},
});

export const logDrillTool = defineTool({
	name: "log_drill_run-the-team",
	description: "Log a drill session for the Run the Team pack. Drills: say-it, organise-corners, blind-defender. Rating 1-5 is the keeper's own feel.",
	parameters: Type.Object({
		drill: Type.Union([Type.Literal("say-it"), Type.Literal("organise-corners"), Type.Literal("blind-defender")]),
		date: Type.String({ description: "ISO date (YYYY-MM-DD)" }),
		rating: Type.Integer({ minimum: 1, maximum: 5 }),
		notes: Type.Optional(Type.String()),
	}),
	execute: async (args, api, context) => {
		const keeperId = keeperIdOf({ conversationId: api.conversationId, agent: await api.agent(context) });
		const event = await api.commit((tx) => appendEvent(tx, keeperId, "drill.logged", { drill: args.drill, date: args.date, rating: args.rating, notes: args.notes ?? "" }, CTX), context);
		return { content: [{ type: "text", text: `recorded drill.logged ${args.drill} ${args.date} rating ${args.rating} (t=${event.t})` }] };
	},
});

export const saveReviewTool = defineTool({
	name: "save_review_run-the-team",
	description: "Save a post-match self-review for the Run the Team pack. Reviews: match-review. One answer per prompt, in prompt order.",
	parameters: Type.Object({
		review: Type.Union([Type.Literal("match-review")]),
		date: Type.String({ description: "ISO date (YYYY-MM-DD) of the match" }),
		answers: Type.Array(Type.String()),
	}),
	execute: async (args, api, context) => {
		const keeperId = keeperIdOf({ conversationId: api.conversationId, agent: await api.agent(context) });
		const id = `r${Date.now().toString(36)}${Math.random().toString(36).slice(2, 7)}`;
		const event = await api.commit((tx) => appendEvent(tx, keeperId, "review.saved", { review: args.review, entry: { id, date: args.date, answers: args.answers } }, CTX), context);
		return { content: [{ type: "text", text: `recorded review.saved ${args.review} ${args.date} id ${id} (t=${event.t})` }] };
	},
});

